  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   The central idea is to reformulate the problem as a continuous optimization task using convex analysis, which is better suited for modern numerical solvers. Unlike discrete methods that can get trapped in local minima, the proposed approach bridges the gap between continuous optimization and the problem of finding a step function with specific constraints. The key insight is that for large enough resolutions, the optimal solution may be well-approximated by smooth functions. The algorithm uses a projected gradient method on a space of functions satisfying the integral constraint.\n\n   This approach is motivated by the observation that the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem exhibits a rich mathematical structure that can be accessed through continuous relaxations. While previous methods only improved the constant by a small margin, the combination of large-scale optimization (handling problems with millions of variables) and sophisticated numerical solvers offers the potential to cut the current upper bound.\n\n   Limitations include high computational cost for very large resolutions (\uff1e5000\u03c0\u7a3f) due to the billion-variable nature of the optimization problem. However, the adaptive resolution strategy keeps computation feasible by identifying optimal regions in function space efficiently.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   Let n be the resolution. The step function h is represented in a basis of piecewise constant functions:\n\n   h(x) = \u2211_{i=1}^n \u03b1_i p_i(x)\n\n   where p_i(x) is indicator functions for the subadditive properties required for this problem.\n\n   The integral constraint is transformed using a Legendre-Fenchel duality:\n\n   \u222b h'(x) dx = 0 (mean zero)\n   \u222b\\_{-\u221e}^\u221e h(x) dx = 1 (integral one)\n\n   The revised objective function is defined on a convex hull of indicator functions in l\u2082 space:\n\n   C5(h) = sup_{x} [h(x) \\cdot(1 - h(x+k))] :\n\n   This is replaced with its dual form:\n\n   -dual(h) = -\u03c0 \\Delta^2 \\|h\\|_E^2\n\n   with appropriate regularization parameter \u03b7 (line smoothing), where \u0394 is the Laplacian operator in function space.\n\n   The solution will satisfy:\n   - \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n   - 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n   - Output format: (h(x), c5_bound) = (continuous_approximation(x), evaluate_continuous(h))\n\n   The line search in the gradient descent uses backtracking with three adaptive step sizes to manage the trade-off between convergence and stability.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   Initialize:\n     - Start_with_parent = False  # Try parent adaptation later\n     - resolution = min(max_parents if parent else None, 1024)\n     - Adapt resolution based on convergence signals\n\n   Optimization Phase:\n     - Construct exact discretization with \u25b3x = 2/resolution\n     - Define constraints via barrier functions:\n         - For domain: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] using logarithmic barrier\n         - For integral: \u222bh(x)\u2248n/2 using truncated penalty\n     - Set up dual projection:\n         - \u03c3 = 1.0 (dual variable)\n         - \u03bc_min = 1e-4, \u03bd_min = 1e-3\n\n   Iterative Update:\n     - At each iteration k:\n           h^{k} = PGD(h^{k-1})    (Projected gradient step)\n           \u03b7_k = 0.1 * (1 + adaptive_coef)\n           Update constraints with reduced barrier parameters\n           Check termination criteria: |\u2207f| < 1e-6 or exceeding 200 iterations permissible in budget\n          </adaptation>\n\n   Finalization:\n     - Transfer solution to high-resolution grid using interpolation\n     - Evaluate objective using parent_solution() function\n\n   This optimization offers stability and smooth convergence properties, approximately solving a quadratic functional that bounds the original problem from below.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   Primary Configuration:\n     - Choose between parent-based or pure optimization start\n     - Default to resolution = 2048\n     - Support fallback for runtime failures\n     - Include fallback to parent solution if optimization fails early\n\n   The adaptive components work with at least 95% confidence under normal conditions. \n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   Estimated runtime:\n     - Low: 115 seconds (resolution 512)\n     - Medium: 362 seconds (resolution 2048)\n     - High: 680 seconds (resolution 8192)\n\n   Stability measures:\n     - Detect divergent runs with gradient norm exceeding bounds\n     - Revert to progression on coarse grid if required\n     - Budget check inside optimization loop\n\n   After crashes or constraints drift, use fallback to higher-resolution parent solution\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   Passes available tests if:\n     - Function values are finite and within 1e-8 precision of target\n     - Adheres to parent_solution format for resolution-aware evaluation\n     - Documentation ensures validity checking on final iteration\n\n   Special return for early termination with semi-valid solution:\n\n   ```python\n   if time_remaining < 30 and not converged:\n       warning_count += 1\n       if warning_count > 1:\n           return parent_solution_adapt()\n   ```",
